[1] In 1968, Spencer Silver, a chemist at the 3M company, was trying to develop a strong adhesive. [2] Instead, he created a weak {{1|one, it}} stuck to surfaces but could be peeled off cleanly and used again. [3] For years, Silver {{2|repeatedly and again and again}} described his discovery to colleagues, but no one could think of a practical use for it. {{3|}}

[4] The solution came from Art Fry, another 3M scientist{{4|, who sang in his church choir}}. [5] The paper slips he used to mark songs in his hymnal {{5|keeps}} falling out, and in 1974 he realized that Silver's adhesive could make a bookmark that stayed in place without damaging the pages.

[6] 3M's first attempt to sell the notes, under the name Press 'n Peel, failed in test markets in 1977. [7] {{6|However,}} when the company gave away free samples in Boise, Idaho, in 1978, most people who tried them {{7|were totally hooked}}. [8] The product was launched across the United States in 1980 as Post-it Notes, and {{8|people liked them}}. [9] {{9|An adhesive that had seemed useless for years had finally found its purpose.}}

[1] During World War II, the U.S. Forest Service worried that wildfires would destroy timber that the nation needed for the war effort. [2] In 1944, working with the Ad Council, the agency introduced a new spokesperson for fire prevention: a bear wearing {{1|jeans, and a campaign hat}}. [3] The first poster showed the bear pouring a bucket of water on a campfire. [4] In 1947, the campaign adopted the slogan "Only you can prevent forest fires."

[5] {{2|Bears are found in many national forests.}} [6] That spring, firefighters battling a blaze in the Capitan Mountains of New Mexico found a badly burned bear cub clinging to a charred tree. {{3|}} [7] The cub, {{4|who}} the firefighters named Smokey, was treated for his burns and sent to the National Zoo in Washington, D.C., where he {{5|lives}} until 1976.

[8] The real Smokey quickly became one of the most recognizable animals in the country, {{6|and many people knew about him}}. [9] In 2001, the campaign {{7|updated and revised}} its slogan to "Only you can prevent wildfires." [10] {{8|Smokey Bear remains a popular figure today.}}
